1
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:08,000
Subtitles added by Hanson Hu

2
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:55,000
Episode 03 - Grandpa's Trunk

3
00:01:50,780 --> 00:01:54,320
Marilyn, you want coffee or tea?

4
00:01:54,320 --> 00:01:55,360
Coffee, please.

5
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:11,140
I am so excited!

6
00:02:11,140 --> 00:02:12,960
At this time tomorrow morning,

7
00:02:12,960 --> 00:02:16,020
Grandpa will be sitting in the kitchen with us.

8
00:02:21,040 --> 00:02:22,320
When does he arrive?

9
00:02:22,860 --> 00:02:24,600
At six o'clock this evening.

10
00:02:25,660 --> 00:02:26,680
By plane?

11
00:02:26,680 --> 00:02:29,100
No, by train.

12
00:02:30,680 --> 00:02:32,620
Are we picking him up at the station?

13
00:02:33,240 --> 00:02:34,680
Not Grandpa.

14
00:02:34,680 --> 00:02:37,200
He doesn't want anybody picking him up.

15
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:39,700
He likes to be independent.

16
00:02:39,700 --> 00:02:44,520
Oh, let's go upstairs and prepare Grandpa's room.

17
00:02:44,520 --> 00:02:46,120
Great! Let's do it!

18
00:02:47,820 --> 00:02:48,480
Good morning, Mom.

19
00:02:48,480 --> 00:02:49,160
Morning.

20
00:02:49,160 --> 00:02:49,500
Well, hi, fellas.

21
00:02:49,500 --> 00:02:50,200
Hello.

22
00:02:50,200 --> 00:02:51,040
Hi, honey.

23
00:02:51,040 --> 00:02:51,920
Morning, Marilyn.

24
00:02:51,920 --> 00:02:53,640
We are going upstairs to set up Grandpa's room.

25
00:02:53,640 --> 00:02:55,000
There's coffee ready.

26
00:02:58,240 --> 00:03:00,320
I'm really excited about seeing Grandpa.

27
00:03:00,540 --> 00:03:03,360
Me, too. Milk, please.

28
00:03:04,100 --> 00:03:05,320
He's so funny.

29
00:03:05,320 --> 00:03:06,800
He always makes me laugh.

30
00:03:09,200 --> 00:03:11,420
I hope Grandpa's going to like living with us.

31
00:03:11,420 --> 00:03:12,520
I think he will.

32
00:03:12,520 --> 00:03:15,520
It just takes time to feel comfortable in a new place.

33
00:03:15,520 --> 00:03:17,080
Won't he miss being in Florida?

34
00:03:17,080 --> 00:03:18,000
Well, he will.

35
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:20,100
But I think he'll like being here with the family.

36
00:03:20,100 --> 00:03:21,600
Are you sure about that?

37
00:03:21,600 --> 00:03:23,280
It's crazy here most of the time.

38
00:03:23,280 --> 00:03:24,520
But it's fun.

39
00:03:24,520 --> 00:03:25,600
That's for sure.

40
00:03:26,560 --> 00:03:26,880
You know,

41
00:03:26,880 --> 00:03:29,640
maybe I'll put together some photos of Grandpa

42
00:03:29,640 --> 00:03:30,600
as a "welcome" present.

43
00:03:30,780 --> 00:03:32,480
That's a neat idea.

44
00:03:32,840 --> 00:03:34,160
What can I do?

45
00:03:34,720 --> 00:03:35,680
I've got it!

46
00:03:35,680 --> 00:03:39,600
I have a picture of Grandpa and Dad and me in my wallet.

47
00:03:40,360 --> 00:03:42,120
It's from the Fathers and Sons' Breakfast

48
00:03:42,120 --> 00:03:44,020
at my junior high school graduation.

49
00:03:44,700 --> 00:03:46,920
Oh, I remember this picture.

50
00:03:48,840 --> 00:03:51,780
I'd really like to pick up Grandpa at the railroad station.

51
00:03:52,320 --> 00:03:54,280
Railroad stations or airports—

52
00:03:54,280 --> 00:03:57,000
Grandpa always tells us he'll get here by himself.

53
00:03:57,000 --> 00:03:59,080
He's something!

54
00:04:03,700 --> 00:04:05,340
Is this all Grandpa's stuff?

55
00:04:06,080 --> 00:04:07,320
That's it.

56
00:04:07,320 --> 00:04:10,600
But I'm sure he has a few bags with him on the train.

57
00:04:12,140 --> 00:04:13,600
What's inside?

58
00:04:15,440 --> 00:04:18,400
I don't know. It's locked.

59
00:04:22,200 --> 00:04:23,560
Hi.

60
00:04:23,560 --> 00:04:24,400
Oh, hi, darling.

61
00:04:24,400 --> 00:04:25,120
Morning, Philip.

62
00:04:25,120 --> 00:04:28,320
I want to put some of my good hangers in Grandpa's closet.

63
00:04:28,320 --> 00:04:31,640
You know I'm very excited about his arrival.

64
00:04:32,300 --> 00:04:33,600
We are, too.

65
00:04:33,600 --> 00:04:35,960
Susan called early this morning.

66
00:04:35,960 --> 00:04:36,940
She's unhappy

67
00:04:36,940 --> 00:04:40,120
because she's had to go to Chicago on a business trip

68
00:04:40,120 --> 00:04:41,520
and can't leave till tonight.

69
00:04:41,520 --> 00:04:43,320
She wants to be here for Grandpa.

70
00:04:43,320 --> 00:04:46,340
Well, Grandpa will be disappointed, too.

71
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:48,520
He loves Susan.

72
00:04:48,520 --> 00:04:50,840
She always reminds him of Grandma.

73
00:04:51,820 --> 00:04:54,440
Well, how's everything here?

74
00:04:54,440 --> 00:04:58,940
Fine. We were just wondering about this trunk.

75
00:05:01,340 --> 00:05:02,080
It's locked.

76
00:05:02,080 --> 00:05:06,720
Oh. I have the key. Grandpa sent it to me.

77
00:05:20,600 --> 00:05:22,760
He doesn't want anybody picking him up.

78
00:05:22,760 --> 00:05:25,140
He likes to be independent.

79
00:05:26,560 --> 00:05:28,760
I think that's good.

80
00:05:29,020 --> 00:05:31,080
He's got his own way of doing things.

81
00:05:31,080 --> 00:05:33,800
He likes to take care of himself.

82
00:05:34,280 --> 00:05:36,040
I guess you're right.

83
00:05:37,040 --> 00:05:38,940
He's so funny.

84
00:05:38,940 --> 00:05:40,400
He always makes me laugh.

85
00:05:40,940 --> 00:05:43,360
I guess he has a good sense of humor.

86
00:05:43,360 --> 00:05:45,840
Yeah, I bet he's a lot of fun.

87
00:05:46,040 --> 00:05:49,060
Grandpa always tells us he'll get here by himself.

88
00:05:49,600 --> 00:05:50,580
He's something.

89
00:05:52,060 --> 00:05:55,380
In my opinion, he's a very special man.

90
00:05:55,440 --> 00:05:56,480
What do you think?

91
00:05:56,480 --> 00:05:59,680
Well, I believe I'm gonna like him. But...

92
00:05:59,780 --> 00:06:00,740
But what?

93
00:06:00,740 --> 00:06:03,360
Won't he miss being in Florida?

94
00:06:03,360 --> 00:06:04,140
Well, he will,

95
00:06:04,140 --> 00:06:06,340
but I think he'll like being here with the family.

96
00:06:06,960 --> 00:06:09,800
I suppose he'll miss his home in Florida

97
00:06:10,040 --> 00:06:11,360
and his independence.

98
00:06:11,840 --> 00:06:13,840
I suppose you're right.

99
00:06:13,920 --> 00:06:15,480
But what about you?

100
00:06:15,480 --> 00:06:17,400
What do you think?

101
00:06:18,040 --> 00:06:20,120
What kind of man is Grandpa?

102
00:06:20,120 --> 00:06:22,120
What do you imagine he's like?

103
00:06:22,440 --> 00:06:24,840
Why is he moving away from Florida?

104
00:06:24,840 --> 00:06:27,960
And how does he feel about moving to Riverdale?

105
00:06:27,960 --> 00:06:29,380
What do you think?

106
00:06:29,440 --> 00:06:30,560
Let's see.

107
00:07:30,480 --> 00:07:32,600
Excuse me. Is this seat taken?

108
00:07:32,600 --> 00:07:33,960
No, it's not taken.

109
00:07:33,960 --> 00:07:35,120
Oh, thank you.

110
00:07:35,120 --> 00:07:36,820
Oh, let me help you with this.

111
00:07:36,820 --> 00:07:38,400
Oh, thank you.

112
00:07:43,420 --> 00:07:44,760
Do you want to sit by the window?

113
00:07:44,760 --> 00:07:47,640
No, no, no. I like the aisle seat better.

114
00:07:47,640 --> 00:07:49,520
Please, you sit by the window.

115
00:07:56,020 --> 00:07:58,280
My name is Stewart... Malcolm Stewart.

116
00:07:58,280 --> 00:07:59,320
Pleased to meet you.

117
00:07:59,320 --> 00:08:01,660
I'm Elsa Tobin. How do you do?

118
00:08:07,400 --> 00:08:09,400
Do you live in New York?

119
00:08:09,400 --> 00:08:11,400
No, no. I'm from Florida.

120
00:08:11,400 --> 00:08:17,000
I am, too. But didn't you just get on?

121
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:19,840
No, no. I just changed my seat.

122
00:08:19,840 --> 00:08:22,240
A man next to me was smoking,

123
00:08:22,240 --> 00:08:25,040
and smoke really bothers me.

124
00:08:27,420 --> 00:08:29,440
Where are you from in Florida?

125
00:08:29,440 --> 00:08:32,360
Titusville. It's near Orlando.

126
00:08:32,360 --> 00:08:34,640
Small world. I'm from Titusville, too.

127
00:08:34,640 --> 00:08:38,460
Really? What part?

128
00:08:38,460 --> 00:08:41,120
My husband and I live near Spaceport.

129
00:08:41,120 --> 00:08:43,800
I know that area.

130
00:08:43,800 --> 00:08:47,340
My house is only a few miles from Spaceport.

131
00:08:48,560 --> 00:08:50,100
Do you still live there?

132
00:08:50,100 --> 00:08:52,600
Oh yes, yes. My husband's there now.

133
00:08:52,600 --> 00:08:57,440
He couldn't take time off to come to New York with me.

134
00:08:58,680 --> 00:09:00,440
Do you still live there?

135
00:09:00,440 --> 00:09:06,400
No. I sold the house and the furniture,

136
00:09:06,400 --> 00:09:10,360
put a few personal things in an old trunk,

137
00:09:10,360 --> 00:09:13,580
and shipped it to my children in New York.

138
00:09:13,880 --> 00:09:15,880
That's my destination.

139
00:09:15,880 --> 00:09:17,340
Are you married?

140
00:09:17,680 --> 00:09:20,680
My wife died four years ago.

141
00:09:21,880 --> 00:09:25,260
She was a wonderful woman. A real friend.

142
00:09:25,980 --> 00:09:29,780
I'm sorry. Really, I'm sorry.

143
00:09:31,120 --> 00:09:33,120
Lots of wonderful memories.

144
00:09:34,260 --> 00:09:36,400
We were married almost fifty years.

145
00:09:36,400 --> 00:09:39,160
Well, forty-seven, to be exact.

146
00:09:39,160 --> 00:09:42,640
John and I celebrate our fortieth anniversary next month.

147
00:09:42,640 --> 00:09:45,820
Oh, congratulations! That's nice.

148
00:09:47,880 --> 00:09:49,880
What does John do?

149
00:09:49,880 --> 00:09:51,940
He's an aerospace engineer

150
00:09:51,940 --> 00:09:54,820
and works for Orlando Aircraft Corporation.

151
00:09:55,100 --> 00:09:59,600
He started with them almost forty years ago.

152
00:10:00,120 --> 00:10:01,440
What do you do?

153
00:10:02,160 --> 00:10:07,120
I just retired. Had my own company.

154
00:10:07,120 --> 00:10:12,580
A construction company. Roads, bridges, big stuff.

155
00:10:13,760 --> 00:10:17,680
But I just sold it and retired.

156
00:10:21,340 --> 00:10:23,200
Excuse me, ma'am. Ticket, please.

157
00:10:26,620 --> 00:10:29,300
Would you kindly hold these keys, please?

158
00:10:30,640 --> 00:10:32,860
I have a ticket, I know.

159
00:10:33,280 --> 00:10:35,280
I was in the smoking section.

160
00:10:35,280 --> 00:10:36,800
It's OK, lady. Take your time.

161
00:10:38,080 --> 00:10:40,840
I'm sure it's in your purse, Mrs. Tobin.

162
00:10:41,280 --> 00:10:44,040
Oh, here it is.

163
00:10:46,720 --> 00:10:49,000
And here are your keys.

164
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:50,060
Thank you.

165
00:10:55,420 --> 00:10:57,780
Do you have family in New York?

166
00:10:57,780 --> 00:11:02,680
No, no. But I do have very close friends in New York City.

167
00:11:03,340 --> 00:11:05,560
We like to go to the theater together.

168
00:11:06,620 --> 00:11:08,680
You said you have family in New York.

169
00:11:08,680 --> 00:11:10,360
Yes, indeed.

170
00:11:10,760 --> 00:11:14,760
A son and his wife and their three children—

171
00:11:14,760 --> 00:11:16,120
my grandchildren.

172
00:11:16,120 --> 00:11:17,440
You must be excited.

173
00:11:18,300 --> 00:11:20,520
I can't wait, to see them!

174
00:11:21,440 --> 00:11:23,040
Are you going to live with them?

175
00:11:23,200 --> 00:11:24,060
Yes.

176
00:11:24,060 --> 00:11:25,120
Permanently?

177
00:11:25,820 --> 00:11:29,580
Well... they want me to,

178
00:11:29,900 --> 00:11:32,440
but it's too early to know for sure.

179
00:11:32,440 --> 00:11:34,160
I'm pretty independent.

180
00:11:35,180 --> 00:11:39,200
I tried to teach my kids the importance of independence,

181
00:11:40,100 --> 00:11:44,040
but I'm not sure I want to be alone.

182
00:11:45,380 --> 00:11:49,840
Some people don't mind being alone. I do.

183
00:11:50,760 --> 00:11:54,560
I understand. But tell me.

184
00:11:54,560 --> 00:11:56,580
Why did you stop working?

185
00:11:57,940 --> 00:12:05,560
I retired because... I wanted to be with my family.

186
00:12:06,080 --> 00:12:09,920
I didn't want to be alone anymore!

187
00:12:35,200 --> 00:12:37,780
What's your name?

188
00:12:39,160 --> 00:12:42,260
My name is Stewart... Malcolm Stewart.

189
00:12:48,640 --> 00:12:51,680
I'm Elsa Tobin. How do you do?

190
00:12:56,880 --> 00:12:58,880
Titusville.

191
00:13:00,520 --> 00:13:03,720
Small world. I'm from Titusville, too.

192
00:13:07,560 --> 00:13:10,640
How do you do?

193
00:13:12,880 --> 00:13:16,480
Are you married, yes or no?

194
00:13:16,680 --> 00:13:19,360
Please tell me, let me know.

195
00:13:19,560 --> 00:13:24,080
John and I celebrate our fortieth anniversary next month.

196
00:13:24,920 --> 00:13:26,620
Congratulations.

197
00:13:27,720 --> 00:13:29,440
Congratulations.

198
00:13:29,640 --> 00:13:33,360
What do you do?

199
00:13:33,360 --> 00:13:37,040
Are you a doctor, a farmer,

200
00:13:37,040 --> 00:13:40,200
a mailman, a lawyer, an artist,

201
00:13:40,200 --> 00:13:43,360
a salesman, a businessman,

202
00:13:43,560 --> 00:13:49,200
or a preacher, a high school teacher?

203
00:13:51,240 --> 00:13:52,920
I just retired.

204
00:13:53,040 --> 00:13:56,480
Do you have family in New York?

205
00:13:56,480 --> 00:13:57,880
No.

206
00:13:58,080 --> 00:14:01,480
Do you have family in New York?

207
00:14:01,560 --> 00:14:02,800
Yes, indeed.

208
00:14:02,880 --> 00:14:06,120
I'm pleased to meet you,

209
00:14:06,320 --> 00:14:09,920
pleased to meet you.

210
00:14:10,840 --> 00:14:12,160
How do you do?

211
00:14:12,200 --> 00:14:15,920
I'm pleased to meet you.

212
00:14:58,260 --> 00:14:59,600
Ladies and gentlemen,

213
00:14:59,600 --> 00:15:02,900
Amtrak is happy to announce our arrival in New York City.

214
00:15:02,900 --> 00:15:05,400
The train will be stopping in five minutes.

215
00:15:05,760 --> 00:15:08,280
Please check to be sure you have your belongings.

216
00:15:08,280 --> 00:15:10,300
And have a good stay in the Big Apple.

217
00:15:10,300 --> 00:15:11,440
Thank you.

218
00:15:11,440 --> 00:15:13,760
Well, here we are.

219
00:15:13,760 --> 00:15:16,400
It was so nice meeting you, Mr. Stewart.

220
00:15:16,400 --> 00:15:19,100
And nice meeting you, too, Mrs. Tobin.

221
00:15:19,560 --> 00:15:20,880
Please look us up.

222
00:15:20,880 --> 00:15:22,200
We're in the phone book.

223
00:15:22,200 --> 00:15:25,280
Dr. Philip Stewart, in Riverdale.

224
00:15:25,280 --> 00:15:26,240
Your son?

225
00:15:26,240 --> 00:15:30,960
That's right. And have a good time in New York.

226
00:15:31,160 --> 00:15:33,480
And don't be so independent.

227
00:15:33,700 --> 00:15:36,380
You're very lucky to have a caring family.

228
00:15:48,680 --> 00:15:50,460
When can we go fishing?

229
00:15:50,460 --> 00:15:53,640
Robbie, we'll go fishing soon,

230
00:15:53,640 --> 00:15:55,640
and we'll take your dad with us.

231
00:15:55,640 --> 00:15:59,220
I'm ready, Grandpa. You name the day.

232
00:15:59,540 --> 00:16:01,780
That's a great idea, Grandpa!

233
00:16:01,780 --> 00:16:03,520
Philip needs a day off.

234
00:16:04,120 --> 00:16:05,960
Let's give him our presents now.

235
00:16:05,960 --> 00:16:07,140
Good idea.

236
00:16:07,300 --> 00:16:09,820
Presents, for me?

237
00:16:12,740 --> 00:16:14,720
From me and Marilyn.

238
00:16:15,140 --> 00:16:16,540
And this one's from me.

239
00:16:16,540 --> 00:16:18,940
I looked all over the house to find it.

240
00:16:34,960 --> 00:16:38,840
Richard, these are terrific pictures.

241
00:16:45,000 --> 00:16:47,860
This one really brings back memories.

242
00:16:50,520 --> 00:16:52,400
You remember that day, Robbie?

243
00:16:52,400 --> 00:16:53,720
I sure do. It was fun.

244
00:16:57,440 --> 00:17:05,600
Oh, I'm sorry Susan isn't here.

245
00:17:05,980 --> 00:17:07,720
I miss her very much.

246
00:17:08,480 --> 00:17:10,780
She feels bad, too, Grandpa.

247
00:17:10,780 --> 00:17:13,040
She called to say the plane was delayed.

248
00:17:13,040 --> 00:17:14,080
You know airports.

249
00:17:14,620 --> 00:17:16,540
I can't wait to see her.

250
00:17:17,520 --> 00:17:20,040
She looks just like Grandma at that age.

251
00:17:23,480 --> 00:17:25,020
I'd better unpack.

252
00:17:25,400 --> 00:17:28,220
I started traveling twenty-four hours ago.

253
00:17:31,820 --> 00:17:34,380
I'm not so young anymore.

254
00:17:35,420 --> 00:17:37,000
Don't you want something to eat?

255
00:17:37,000 --> 00:17:39,180
No, thanks.

256
00:17:39,180 --> 00:17:42,520
After a good night's sleep, I'll enjoy breakfast even more.

257
00:17:42,520 --> 00:17:44,160
Well, come on, Dad.

258
00:17:44,160 --> 00:17:45,860
Ellen and I'll take you to your room.

259
00:17:45,860 --> 00:17:47,940
I'm sure glad you're here, Grandpa.

260
00:17:47,940 --> 00:17:49,440
Good night, Grandpa.

261
00:17:49,440 --> 00:17:50,720
Pleasant dreams.

262
00:18:05,680 --> 00:18:09,140
Philip, do you have the key to the trunk?

263
00:18:09,440 --> 00:18:12,440
I have the key, but it doesn't work.

264
00:18:18,520 --> 00:18:21,640
I sent the wrong key.

265
00:18:32,640 --> 00:18:37,800
I have something for you. I made it myself.

266
00:18:39,240 --> 00:18:40,900
I think you'll enjoy it.

267
00:18:42,160 --> 00:18:45,240
I researched it for over a year.

268
00:18:45,240 --> 00:18:47,560
It's our family tree.

269
00:18:51,440 --> 00:18:55,680
Oh, Grandpa! How exciting!

270
00:18:55,680 --> 00:18:57,120
Fabulous!

271
00:18:57,120 --> 00:19:02,040
Why, I didn't know that your grandfather was born in Germany.

272
00:19:02,040 --> 00:19:05,280
Lots of interesting information about our family.

273
00:19:05,560 --> 00:19:07,460
A gift from me.

274
00:19:08,340 --> 00:19:10,760
Thank you so much.

275
00:19:10,760 --> 00:19:12,960
Grandpa! Grandpa!

276
00:19:12,960 --> 00:19:17,800
Oh, Grandpa, I'm so happy to see you!

277
00:19:17,800 --> 00:19:23,820
Oh, you look so beautiful, Susan. My granddaughter.

278
00:19:23,820 --> 00:19:28,480
Like I always said, you look just like Grandma.

279
00:19:28,680 --> 00:19:33,100
I think you're going to be very happy here with us.

280
00:19:33,100 --> 00:19:35,560
I know you will.

281
00:19:35,720 --> 00:19:38,400
I don't feel alone anymore.

282
00:19:55,160 --> 00:19:58,680
Malcolm Stewart is not alone anymore, that's for sure.

283
00:19:58,920 --> 00:20:03,460
In fact, he'll have his family around him every day of his life now.

284
00:20:03,460 --> 00:20:07,160
Now, finding some peace and quiet may be a little difficult,

285
00:20:07,160 --> 00:20:11,440
but my father won't be lonely anymore.

286
00:20:12,280 --> 00:20:16,680
He has me, Philip Stewart, nearby, and the rest of his family.

287
00:20:20,680 --> 00:20:22,120
There's Ellen, his daughter-in-law.

288
00:20:24,760 --> 00:20:28,120
You know, I met Ellen when I was a student at the University of Michigan.

289
00:20:29,100 --> 00:20:30,480
Later, she became my wife.

290
00:20:33,720 --> 00:20:35,180
We have three children now.

291
00:20:37,120 --> 00:20:39,080
Our oldest son is a photographer.

292
00:20:40,840 --> 00:20:42,520
Richard is very talented.

293
00:20:44,440 --> 00:20:45,780
Like his grandfather.

294
00:20:50,220 --> 00:20:51,800
Marilyn is talented, too.

295
00:20:51,800 --> 00:20:52,760
She designs clothing.

296
00:20:53,640 --> 00:20:54,840
Marilyn is Richard's wife.

297
00:20:57,300 --> 00:21:00,240
And she's also a sister-in-law to Susan.

298
00:21:03,700 --> 00:21:05,040
Susan is my daughter.

299
00:21:08,120 --> 00:21:13,440
Susan reminds us of her grandmother, Bernice Cassidy Stewart.

300
00:21:15,160 --> 00:21:15,920
My mother.

301
00:21:18,200 --> 00:21:22,580
She was kind, intelligent, and lovable, like Susan.

302
00:21:26,160 --> 00:21:30,120
Susan has two brothers, Richard and Robert.

303
00:21:30,720 --> 00:21:31,840
We call him Robbie.

304
00:21:32,500 --> 00:21:33,560
He's our other son.

305
00:21:33,600 --> 00:21:40,320
He loves his grandfather very much.

306
00:21:42,000 --> 00:21:44,760
Well, that's our family, the Stewarts.

307
00:21:46,680 --> 00:21:48,000
Did you follow me?

308
00:21:48,560 --> 00:21:49,000
Let's see.

309
00:21:49,440 --> 00:21:50,680
Who is my daughter?

310
00:21:54,520 --> 00:21:56,080
That's it. Susan is my daughter.

311
00:21:57,300 --> 00:21:58,040
How about this?

312
00:21:58,260 --> 00:22:00,040
Who is Robbie's grandfather?

313
00:22:03,800 --> 00:22:05,320
Malcolm is Robbie's grandfather.

314
00:22:06,840 --> 00:22:07,240
Okay.

315
00:22:07,780 --> 00:22:09,580
Who is Susan's sister-in-law?

316
00:22:12,440 --> 00:22:14,940
Marilyn is Susan's sister-in-law.

317
00:22:14,940 --> 00:22:15,560
That's right.

318
00:22:17,420 --> 00:22:20,700
Well, that's our family, and I love them all.

